-- DICT-SEMANTICS §6 C1 + §3 "Specificity": "Two instances with α-equal heads are
-- ⊑-equivalent yet distinct, which C1 (§6) rejects as ambiguous -- DUPLICATE
-- HEADS NEVER TIE-BREAK."
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived): `match(IE, Tag Int)` = both impls. They are
-- mutually ⊑ (identity substitution both ways), so there is no UNIQUE ⊑-minimal
-- element and hence no minimum. `inst` is inapplicable and the program is
-- REJECTED as ambiguous overlap -- "most-specific-wins is a total tie-break, not
-- a licence to pick".
--
-- OBSERVED: check/run/build all REJECT with `T-CONFLICTING-IMPL`. CONFORMANT.
--
-- This is the floor beneath the whole overlap regime: an implementation that
-- silently picked one (first-match, declaration order) would be nondeterministic
-- across resolution paths, which is exactly #203.
--
-- ⚠️ THE ROW F-3d (#614/#311) DELIBERATELY DID NOT WIDEN, and the one whose reject
-- has no second line of defence. F-3d demoted DICT §6.1 condition **(a)** to a
-- warning -- but α-equal heads SATISFY (a) (they are ⊑-COMPARABLE, mutually so), and
-- §6.1.2's own ⚠️ records that the ladder breaks exactly here: **(a) ⇏ (c)**. So this
-- pair is not an (a)-violation at all and was never (a)'s to demote; it is a §6 C1
-- violation, and C1 is not relaxed. Nor could the goal-site min⊑ reject pick it up:
-- `entryCovers` is `tyHeadEqV || tyStrictlyMoreSpecificV`, so two equal heads COVER
-- EACH OTHER, `findMostSpecificEntry` returns `Some`, and `reportAmbiguousOverlap`
-- is never reached. Demoting this arm with the rest would therefore have accepted a
-- program the spec rejects, decided by declaration order, with NOTHING left to
-- report it -- which is why the demotion is classified rather than wholesale.
interface Tag a where
  tag : a -> Int

impl Tag Int where
  tag _ = 1

impl Tag Int where
  tag _ = 2

main = println (tag (1 : Int))
